#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "dcd503a8-a9c6-524b-a8ac-0641ce3e1107" :question/math-academy-id "e-12423" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,2), (0,1)$ and $(1,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of $S',$ the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T?$\n\n- $(-2,0)$\n\n- $(0,0)$\n\n- $(2,0)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of $S'$ are $(-2,0),$ $(0,0),$ and $(2,0).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I, II,  and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "964efb4a-6e6b-5fc1-a993-20aa2b0acdaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-12424" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n  Find the image of the triangle shown below under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/853fd7d5c09944a5bacd0c2b3453504f3fa1eea9d8d3b2c3bdd4f0f44889dc16.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(3,-6), (1,-2),$ and $(-2,4).$  Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/3fd1073b2eaa5aa9c21e537fcc4dc6cf538ab2992c0732f64da50dbcb63d8fd4.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "4aa087bf-ee6c-5e90-9431-c4be6b207333" :question/math-academy-id "e-12438" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/89efcd0ecb51dac08a1102c5a347eea3459f7209de7cbac0a88b192259ca3b7e.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $\\mathbf{T}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307348 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a60886ab-a9e4-5c51-8ce4-015666d95c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307350} {:answer/id #uuid "dd857f6a-0056-526a-8bd7-e08cb1377977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/8f43f19bbe4213ba2f9d5bc51f52b747e1c7840a27607207f0ca56021c1914d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307351} {:answer/id #uuid "bdd696d9-06d4-5a69-bb5e-d38cd9f6d604" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/0f0589a467b3d35947a6f501ff00d5296e05ae7e375800659986038db55c7577.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307352} {:answer/id #uuid "7a38e9d6-28b6-54a1-8f99-6a0757341358" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/378a24a8553ac7092b85f715394bf632b5e87aaf45c58ce3ff7ae66310b28f65.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307353} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8d6760-24d7-594e-af6e-1901cfb5b927" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/95fbb1a2298ebc809303d6c57a8440483cdd65748b090ea0b45d25ebad93bb80.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a60886ab-a9e4-5c51-8ce4-015666d95c02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307350} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3aee704-3c44-51a1-b1a0-55744186f527" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "babac56b-1bc2-59da-833f-f3070de287aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-147603" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/0cec723bf4e6886ca0715e9e02c658a7ee0e1f24555ea3946f95aa2b12b863be.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,2)$, $(-2,2),$ $(2,-2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/c688b1ee540e732329e597fb97a0595f619dbc4ff0aad86b6a01f1bdb51ae66b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ea07bb8-28a5-5b21-8daa-e4cc34c8a4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307357} {:answer/id #uuid "0283521f-3bd0-5b10-a724-d44a0f5ec938" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307358} {:answer/id #uuid "d9f1cf54-d8b8-5ab6-bddb-4f7dc088114a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307359} {:answer/id #uuid "81a68a26-c735-5b55-96f9-76c53053ec22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307360} {:answer/id #uuid "d3389f61-d2a1-5488-9abe-13c48c5f0b1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9f1cf54-d8b8-5ab6-bddb-4f7dc088114a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307359} :answer-field/id #uuid "12f3fe25-accc-5a2c-a20c-33ff19f5e3d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c945cf9e-ace3-510d-9408-69a21f196b20" :question/math-academy-id "q-147667" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -10\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,-1)$, $(1,0)$ and $(-2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$ \n\n- $(-1,2)$\n- $(-3,12)$\n- $(7,-12)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -1 & 7 \\\\ 12 & 2 & -14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-6,12)$, $(-1,2)$ and $(7,-14).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I  only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284110 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f35c6757-c1cc-5e95-adc9-7f3b43987bee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284112} {:answer/id #uuid "f3aab5aa-276e-5326-bbfe-4b9e1ca11ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284113} {:answer/id #uuid "58ad1e6b-adf3-52cd-aecb-ac30e59feb28" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284114} {:answer/id #uuid "cda1164d-4de5-56fe-ab02-1b31eb5243b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284115} {:answer/id #uuid "73c06a27-23a8-5503-bac0-b74613d69742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3aab5aa-276e-5326-bbfe-4b9e1ca11ad8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284113} :answer-field/id #uuid "be65ef3d-5052-5673-a3d9-6e70ca192993" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "88e719bc-e349-5ca0-a13c-a121f56b9351" :question/math-academy-id "q-147668" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,0)$, $(0,-3)$ and $(-2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(12,-24)$\n- $(8,16)$\n- $(2,-4)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 12 & -8 \\\\ -4 & -24 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(2,-4)$, $(12,-24)$ and $(-8,16).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee5af7d6-ac78-5d00-8c37-da4bcfdf7a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284119} {:answer/id #uuid "820502db-e4d6-55f6-9fdc-553dfcad78b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284120} {:answer/id #uuid "838c1b7a-be1e-5ec4-b336-551b12185475" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284121} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee21e47-6f22-5717-8570-01e475c9354a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284122} {:answer/id #uuid "635d14e7-39d4-5aa7-b218-5ecef799c9bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee5af7d6-ac78-5d00-8c37-da4bcfdf7a70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284119} :answer-field/id #uuid "41075aa9-0276-571b-a372-d1c938d5d900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6364a192-b48f-5703-8297-b5247d60439d" :question/math-academy-id "q-147671" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(-1,-1),(0,2),$ and $(-2,1).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(6,-1)$\n- $(7,-14)$\n- $(8,-16)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 \\\\ 10 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 & 7 \\\\ -16 & 12 & -14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(8,-16),$ $(-6,12)$, and $(7,-14).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105992 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96613aa1-9c36-500a-a41e-c35cb51432aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102200} {:answer/id #uuid "bbf8242c-8fb2-5898-ad17-c871c5f73bf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102201} {:answer/id #uuid "ce929ac1-17f1-5017-a524-3020f0f83977" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102202} {:answer/id #uuid "e7aa19f6-551c-5953-ba44-dd77177a2953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102203} {:answer/id #uuid "fb67936e-1532-50e7-8865-84bea44bb18c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7aa19f6-551c-5953-ba44-dd77177a2953" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102203} :answer-field/id #uuid "699e658e-8932-5996-9d7e-35e0ceaaf3fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102199}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6dc04420-7edf-5061-a2f1-81e1e58efd27" :question/math-academy-id "q-162145" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3)-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bb34d14-4e6b-5921-bbae-0804ad06b4f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102206} {:answer/id #uuid "b2b33753-c1ca-54f2-b9c6-98ba27f6fa8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102207} {:answer/id #uuid "cb1ac350-98a0-5f6c-8490-7f08a97c5de0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102208} {:answer/id #uuid "1338e2d6-476a-5cc4-a9f7-69b9fb7f0f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102209} {:answer/id #uuid "39e1c3d2-cf9c-5c0f-ac61-180359ad5ac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1338e2d6-476a-5cc4-a9f7-69b9fb7f0f6c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102209} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d6bd43-1322-57b8-8f87-0e031cdaba61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba678a53-c013-5d44-b391-2d5cc0a9cc96" :question/math-academy-id "q-162150" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/e2ab6906100a96a123b4b1520613f1e38fe2c7e77af390a8efe0596a2861727d.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4437aa5a-a749-5c6d-8f3d-d31b01601abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284140} {:answer/id #uuid "f85eb3db-c70b-5e67-bd54-ecfc8961e9fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284141} {:answer/id #uuid "214d698e-c823-548f-ae58-51ab4fe4e6f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284142} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd42b68-f6b9-5024-b293-d78a5df92981" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284143} {:answer/id #uuid "bcd14e6c-96a8-5e25-8afa-97f7ad9c26f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4437aa5a-a749-5c6d-8f3d-d31b01601abb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284140} :answer-field/id #uuid "beca3bfc-6192-5bd4-9d93-1a9d76036bc8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a34e82b-455b-568a-911e-4e41e8de38c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-162201" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/cfcbd4b374456b4a5913289592d875a42caf81fd02984180e68e34c5b82ef905.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nSince $T$ is the zero matrix, the image of our object under the action of $T$ is the origin."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289303 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b673724a-4915-517c-b9a0-fe79b8e7faa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289305} {:answer/id #uuid "aec1f854-7d74-50f2-a59b-e2ff15f06aa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289306} {:answer/id #uuid "7b50d5f9-72ad-547c-a124-76efa4e14d33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289307} {:answer/id #uuid "c1bf9be5-b982-5166-9542-007bcdd73298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289308} {:answer/id #uuid "24847843-649b-595c-82b8-e8e87f70614a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289309}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1bf9be5-b982-5166-9542-007bcdd73298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289308} :answer-field/id #uuid "e42fdd2c-3928-5581-8f96-17bc27665aed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5cfe0cab-3f64-5782-9a2c-dd556be2b484" :question/math-academy-id "q-162202" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/8978d3bb02b4032b6e3a8b4a85878db647eb8323ac5b0423b5a9a6fab121c7e7.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -8\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-8)-4\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. Therefore, the image of the triangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307362 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1baa123-433a-5455-93e2-a61e991b8bea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The origin $O$ of the coordinate system" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307364} {:answer/id #uuid "1e910840-1c7f-5a28-8855-dcae3fa3112e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A rectangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307365} {:answer/id #uuid "90d87b2d-3e8d-5184-af36-2ba2ebae24d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307366} {:answer/id #uuid "046bba86-0abd-56c8-98ef-335f9918f806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A line segment" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307367} {:answer/id #uuid "c80c2eef-c71b-516a-98ae-4c7e262a15bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A point, different from the origin" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307368}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90d87b2d-3e8d-5184-af36-2ba2ebae24d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "A triangle" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307366} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1e5efe6-9352-5709-abbe-02926409ce77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "875bea68-bfbb-5cce-a8c9-893099395a11" :question/math-academy-id "q-162207" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/57948af956c9b83ed944fafc26e81a5c003401f753bc520e088ffdbd71915c3a.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat would be the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any linear transformation $T$ with associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., $det(T)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., $det(T)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nComputing the determinant of $T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)-(-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a nonsingular matrix. Therefore, the image of our object under the action of  $T$ is a triangle."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8667b4d3-e8da-5b51-86c7-f5069af1dd99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102724} {:answer/id #uuid "96de2d77-24db-56d3-87b9-8efb5afe88c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102725} {:answer/id #uuid "e6db357f-700a-5a75-a186-2aa1fa365131" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102726} {:answer/id #uuid "1f067af3-e8d4-5366-829b-67c6e031edf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102727} {:answer/id #uuid "69645bba-ebbe-5cdf-afe7-c90944267b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96de2d77-24db-56d3-87b9-8efb5afe88c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1351/Source/Images/q-21061-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102725} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b453d2c-355f-59f3-be5b-1ca89843e93f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87f1b1b7-4122-5900-82fa-45ed68940b46" :question/math-academy-id "q-21061" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/5c0196b13b3f20ba05313a9604e73b13251937b525a12fd5b9997e99f4ae21a0.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-1,1)$, $(1,-1),$ $(-1,1).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/2f8d6ec6c9a71e61180ad04c25d623c40dadfdd623e1e2eaa8458915bcaca2f8.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284152 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf7717b0-e75c-518c-8cd3-150b96c8cabc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/dc5ad781e838e597706600633701871006cd59fd3db60344cf34aaddb4200da8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284154} {:answer/id #uuid "9db75bfc-fe19-51e4-ab27-cf8792f76f05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/40f352980b38be63b071b160953eff23a98996dce58c5f616e10958a8a70182e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284155} {:answer/id #uuid "ec7cdc35-c43f-5b8a-adf1-45e56f6e2a2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/92beb71e7d09cba01f2eeb82a60ca3b0b3929064e4f16da3af104db8c5eaa8df.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284156} {:answer/id #uuid "34880a8f-4354-522a-8d0d-3ed1d347e87c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/ee4a96b2b1106fbd1a5ec222c3f7f2a5e5a9e84c9bfe539aeb29a1918943aef9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284157} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4b8ff3-6f9e-5e75-9a7e-86d7a7b55fde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/b4d2150d92ff14a654fb7079a17d7bd75abe721c0d37d4075d5a92635bafc170.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34880a8f-4354-522a-8d0d-3ed1d347e87c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993138/assets/ee4a96b2b1106fbd1a5ec222c3f7f2a5e5a9e84c9bfe539aeb29a1918943aef9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284157} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aeb8c20-4274-5475-9a2f-7e464fc745e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa19c1ea-c0d3-5f29-b074-4ccd448a8f88" :question/math-academy-id "q-21063" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/6501b1a7e9429c786905c11835410111c32ac0337f1ba63bff130d327350316c.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 8 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 4 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(6,3)$, $(8,4)$ and $(10,5).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/a91af151d3dc0e48aef0eef58012f6c9f72aaff02aafae2d55fe0714c29c96e3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289317 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "577bd934-421d-54ec-b0b2-d003700ec403" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/461ff9fb5ef66239eb81314f761fac9ceb32b3bc88df6940b61445c752fe3d0c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289319} {:answer/id #uuid "700107e3-638e-5589-9221-901c6ac2b7d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/bf7cc4e728cd1ade741c55b2fbb54720220e0e09a8521a150300532b4e3ee9ed.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289320} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb774ed-95c1-54d0-a9ab-0cbb1b865b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/ed8a2354f8cc04f998875f599a52b50e6c64cafe8976f1bea176772543e295ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289321} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9411e7-77cf-54e3-a181-f1015dfa57ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0af059340bdef0548876baad9bcdf990f6bc92330617dde33c18c00578aecad1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289322} {:answer/id #uuid "eb1006cf-3407-53b0-b4de-08d098a9bd1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/da61a7fd99ce683be4e66d3708ddf15c969b038f0250dc4c024ace870c3f7544.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf9411e7-77cf-54e3-a181-f1015dfa57ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14009909/assets/0af059340bdef0548876baad9bcdf990f6bc92330617dde33c18c00578aecad1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289322} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c0ee37d-c540-532c-b043-87ace79e55ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289318}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f28ce8d4-0044-5e40-a90c-5299f41f2c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-21065" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/db7d2b33f2badad28b754d4482047a33bb37469ed8b7b17859b10552840e2f51.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 12 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(-2,4)$, \n$(-6,12)$ and $(-1,2).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/2e1dde461c6244a59672fc28c842b70a30297ac7a8c7e7cf2095a973cd1860cd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e573bd12-c69d-585f-b2f1-b7cc58615357" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307371} {:answer/id #uuid "051bda51-67d7-535e-99b7-492d1527b90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307372} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf3cbb3-ce70-5cd6-ae1c-ed9f20361d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307373} {:answer/id #uuid "b172fe44-e39d-56ae-8721-1b78f822cc89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307374} {:answer/id #uuid "7cd447ed-e12e-5187-800a-3a907ea67047" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "051bda51-67d7-535e-99b7-492d1527b90c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307372} :answer-field/id #uuid "bfec58de-eab4-58db-9cfa-0469208367f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea75fda7-a514-5ed4-bcda-72806b8641ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-21068" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(1,2)$, $(-1,2)$ and $(-1,0)$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(16,8)$\n- $(8,2)$\n- $(-4,4)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$ \n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & 8 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(16,8)$, $(8,4)$ and $(-4,-2).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307376 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90c88263-67ad-5b92-a0f3-90331ef7eb5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307378} {:answer/id #uuid "6bfb653d-877d-5d5b-a53c-869a8bd060d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307379} {:answer/id #uuid "dc18865f-c3ea-51c9-bb18-0a82b54c29f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307380} {:answer/id #uuid "170bc223-b505-5cb8-bbc7-4771033478bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307381} {:answer/id #uuid "c826b540-a50c-57cf-9a12-04772e252dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c826b540-a50c-57cf-9a12-04772e252dbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307382} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa4a3859-985b-54af-b2f1-ecb0b3eb0cb5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1aacc7ee-838d-5fb9-bee5-d3a0b7547a65" :question/math-academy-id "q-96856" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA triangle $S$ has vertices at $(0,-1),(1,1),$ and $(3,2).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the action of $T?$\n\n- $(-1,4)$\n- $(-2,4)$\n- $(1,-3)$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are  $(-2,4)$, and $(1,-2).$ \n\nSo, the correct answer is \" II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186307383 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a213db2-99af-5363-acd0-6a26351bab33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/70d88ffc1ab7dfe2813e73d0106e913d3a1a14975d2d765ebfbd27ff3079eae1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307385} {:answer/id #uuid "6f216a7f-7a71-5658-885a-ceeab087a258" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/aa4ec8c9bbf65797904b60a87f950113b4d720dcce16fc7ace3f349169df83ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307386} {:answer/id #uuid "ef237fa8-2614-52af-b475-5dfb72c6efdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/e3759f48aad9f2b69a76ff0979858e63b464facdad692c75709e989e31c01466.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307387} {:answer/id #uuid "ed91807a-6876-5ff1-86cb-cbee1c7cf301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/3837eb3e6aaf4ee7fbd5c0d80d588e106d7f13958cd6e9c96dcc8784e8c460e7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307388} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea5bc46-d342-58e6-a356-c4195f0a6431" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/8218cbbfcd19517e45233cb53649a0daf07cd094defcfd925e9d0b4c48746ccb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a213db2-99af-5363-acd0-6a26351bab33" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/70d88ffc1ab7dfe2813e73d0106e913d3a1a14975d2d765ebfbd27ff3079eae1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307385} :answer-field/id #uuid "0159cddf-dae8-5c37-977c-de7bd01e7d64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186307384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ca3fcdf-3fc3-510a-b699-c636d97b2e93" :question/math-academy-id "q-97022" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/699e6ce441757dd21b01524b5405ab7f4b596bfe9ec6f8d0532a1e6f112eb8ad.png)\n\nConsider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is the image of the triangle shown above under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the image of the triangle under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of the triangle:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(1,2)$, $(2,4),$ $(3,6).$ Notice that all three points lie on the same line.\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $T$ is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040372/assets/791c82648482ef9932562150659ef74bb55df0216047ad56bb84f9bcf1b6ce4d.png)"}]}
